1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:07,584
Suppose a neural network has to output a number. Say, the x coordinate of one point on a vector shape.

2
00:00:08,033 --> 00:00:14,163
The obvious approach is regression. Output the number directly, and penalize its distance to the label.

3
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:22,170
A popular alternative sounds almost wasteful. Chop the range into bins, and treat the problem as classification.

4
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:29,891
Why would throwing away precision ever help? To see it, we have to look at what each loss is actually asking for.

5
00:00:31,367 --> 00:00:40,748
Freeze the input, and look at every training label that goes with it. Here they're tightly clustered. The prediction, this yellow marker, is a single number.

6
00:00:41,167 --> 00:00:54,263
Squared error, or L2, connects the prediction to every label with a spring. The farther away a label is, the harder its spring pulls. The prediction settles where all the pulls cancel out. That's the mean.

7
00:00:54,700 --> 00:01:04,545
Now make the labels ambiguous. For the very same input, seventy percent of the labels say the point is up here, and thirty percent say it's down there.

8
00:01:04,967 --> 00:01:11,654
The springs still cancel at the mean, and the mean lands right in the gap. A value that no label ever had.

9
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:22,688
This isn't a training bug. Plot the average loss for every possible prediction, and the bottom of the bowl really is in the gap. L2 found exactly what it was asked for.

10
00:01:24,167 --> 00:01:33,501
Absolute error, or L1, swaps the springs for ropes, and every rope pulls with the same force, no matter how far away its label is.

11
00:01:33,933 --> 00:01:43,268
So it becomes a vote. Twenty eight ropes pull right, twelve pull left, and the prediction gets dragged all the way into the majority cluster. That's the median.

12
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:54,799
But at fifty fifty, the vote is tied everywhere in between. The loss goes completely flat, and the prediction just drifts wherever initialization and noise happen to leave it.

13
00:01:55,233 --> 00:02:10,094
Here's the same story as a picture of how hard each loss pushes. L2's push grows with the error, so one wild outlier can dominate. L1 pushes equally hard at any distance. Huber loss is spring-like up close, and rope-like far away.

14
00:02:11,567 --> 00:02:22,248
Classification does something different. Cut the range into bins, and give every bin its own score. A softmax turns those scores into probabilities.

15
00:02:22,667 --> 00:02:32,280
Cross entropy rewards putting probability on whichever bin the label falls into. Each label nudges up its own bin, and nothing else.

16
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:44,449
So there's no tug of war. The seventy percent and the thirty percent push on different bins, and both peaks simply grow. The network learns the whole histogram.

17
00:02:44,867 --> 00:02:57,034
One refinement. Instead of a one-hot target, spread each label over its neighbors as a small Gaussian. That tells the network that neighboring bins are almost the same answer.

18
00:02:58,533 --> 00:03:07,078
But in the end we still need one number. If we take the most likely bin, the argmax, we commit to one peak, and that is always a real answer.

19
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,023
If instead we take the expected value, the probability weighted average, we're finding the balance point of the histogram.

20
00:03:15,467 --> 00:03:21,132
And the balance point is the mean. Right back in the gap. Exactly the L2 answer.

21
00:03:21,567 --> 00:03:33,502
The expected value isn't useless, though. Inside a single peak it interpolates between bins, and it's more precise than the argmax. So the best recipe is: pick the peak, then average within it.

22
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:51,254
Now let's see it on real training runs. Until now we froze a single input. Here, the input x varies along the horizontal axis, and the label y is on the vertical axis.

23
00:03:51,700 --> 00:04:03,171
Each vertical slice of this plot is one of the number lines from before. Take the slice at x equals zero point eight seven five. Its labels pile up in two places: two possible answers.

24
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:14,838
The plot has four regions. First, one clean answer. Then one answer with a few junk labels. Then two answers, seventy thirty. And finally two answers, fifty fifty.

25
00:04:15,267 --> 00:04:25,716
Here's the network trained with L2. It's fine while there's one answer. But as soon as there are two, it falls into the gap between them, just like the springs predicted.

26
00:04:26,133 --> 00:04:32,774
Trained with L1, it follows the majority answer at seventy thirty, but at fifty fifty it wanders.

27
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:44,438
The bin head predicts a whole histogram at every x. Brighter means more probability. It lights up both curves. On our slice, it has two peaks, right where the labels are.

28
00:04:44,867 --> 00:04:53,783
Its argmax picks one peak at each x. In the fifty fifty region it jumps back and forth, but every point it picks is on a real curve.

29
00:04:54,200 --> 00:05:10,129
Here's how far each prediction lands from the nearest correct answer, in the two ambiguous regions. L2 is off by zero point one seven. L1 by zero point zero four. The bins by zero point zero zero two. About a hundred times better.

30
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:20,749
A practical question: what if you need thousands of bins? One trick is to pick in two steps. First, which of sixteen coarse bins.

31
00:05:21,167 --> 00:05:25,300
Then zoom into that coarse bin, and pick one of sixteen fine bins inside it.

32
00:05:25,733 --> 00:05:39,480
If every coarse bin gets its own fine histogram, this is exactly one big softmax with two hundred fifty six bins, just split into two steps. Nothing is lost.

33
00:05:39,900 --> 00:05:56,386
A cheaper version uses one fine histogram for all coarse bins. Here's the catch. Our two answers sit in different coarse bins, and at different spots inside them. Answer A is near the left of its bin. Answer B is near the right of its bin.

34
00:05:56,833 --> 00:06:09,929
A single shared fine histogram has to hold both spots at once. So we can end up with answer A's coarse bin, combined with answer B's spot. That point is neither answer.

35
00:06:10,367 --> 00:06:15,614
So, give each coarse bin its own fine histogram, or just use one big softmax.

36
00:06:17,100 --> 00:06:26,620
Now let's move to two dimensions, which is closer to your vector shapes. Picture the letter O. It has an outer ring, and an inner ring.

37
00:06:27,067 --> 00:06:36,122
The input is a position along the path. The output is a point, with an x and a y coordinate. Usually, the label is a point on the outer ring.

38
00:06:36,567 --> 00:06:45,483
But in this one quarter, it's ambiguous. Half of the labels put the point on the outer ring, and half put it on the inner ring.

39
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:54,306
L2 averages the two, and lands halfway between the rings. Do that all the way around, and L2 draws a ring that isn't in the letter at all.

40
00:06:54,733 --> 00:07:01,746
The bin head lands on the real rings. Well, mostly. Let's zoom into the ambiguous quarter.

41
00:07:02,167 --> 00:07:15,588
This is one input, in that quarter. The model has one histogram for x, along the bottom, and a separate histogram for y, on the left. Each has two peaks: one for the outer ring, and one for the inner ring.

42
00:07:16,033 --> 00:07:30,337
Because x and y are picked separately, any combination is possible. That's four points. Two of them are real. The other two take the outer ring's x and the inner ring's y. They're ghosts, on neither ring.

43
00:07:30,767 --> 00:07:39,219
A single histogram over x and y together only puts probability on the two real points. The ghosts are gone.

44
00:07:40,700 --> 00:07:54,818
Back to your vector model. Each query predicts a whole shape. Early in training, the Hungarian matching keeps changing its mind. In one batch, this query is matched to the circle. In the next, to the square.

45
00:07:55,267 --> 00:08:03,672
That's the same ambiguity as before, now for a whole shape. Regression averages the two targets, and predicts a blob that is neither shape.

46
00:08:04,100 --> 00:08:11,298
A bin head can keep both shapes as possibilities while the matching settles, and then commit to one.

47
00:08:11,733 --> 00:08:24,458
So. L2 predicts the mean. L1 predicts the median. Bins learn the whole distribution. Then decode with care: take the peak, refine inside it, and watch out for ghosts.

